    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Radius scale</h2>
        <p>Corner rounding is <b>one token, not many</b>, so <code>--radius</code> in the tokens file is the only value you edit:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>The sizes behind Tailwind's <code>rounded-*</code> utilities are <em>derived multiples</em> of it. <code>--radius-lg</code> is the base, smaller steps shrink it (0.6×, 0.8×) and larger steps grow it (1.4× to 2.6×).</li>
            <li>Each component picks a step (a badge uses <code>rounded-sm</code>, a card <code>rounded-lg</code>, a dialog <code>rounded-xl</code>), so the proportions between them stay fixed.</li>
            <li>Changing the base re-rounds the whole UI consistently: <code>0</code> reads square, <code>0.5rem</code> subtle, <code>1rem</code> soft.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Theming/Radius" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Dark mode &amp; the system preference</h2>
        <p>Dark mode works by toggling a single class on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b><code>.dark</code> overrides the same tokens</b>, so there is no second component set and no duplicated styles.</li>
            <li><b>The theme module drives it.</b> <code>_content/blaizio.base/dist/theme.js</code> toggles the class, persists the preference (light, dark, or <em>system</em> to follow the OS) in <code>localStorage</code>, and tracks <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> live while in system mode.</li>
            <li><b>No flash of the wrong mode.</b> The small classic script below sits in your host page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and re-applies the persisted choice before Blazor starts.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Theming/Boot" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Programmatic control with IThemeService</h2>
        <p>You don't need the <a href="docs/components/theme-switcher">Theme Switcher</a> component to change the mode. Inject <code>IThemeService</code>, which <code>AddBlaizio()</code> registers, and set the same persisted options from C#: the mode (<code>light</code> / <code>dark</code> / <code>system</code>) and the reading direction.</p>
        <p><code>SetStyleAsync</code> toggles a <code>style-*</code> class. That only matters in setups that load several styles, like this docs site. An installed project ships one style inlined in its components and switches it with <code>blaizio apply</code>.</p>
        <p>Changes made through the service and through a switcher stay in sync, because both go through the same module and both raise <code>ThemeChanged</code>. The service is backed by JS interop, so call it from <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> or event handlers, never during prerendering.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Theming/ThemeService" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Customizing tokens</h2>
        <p>Edit the values directly in the tokens file, <code>Styles/app.css</code>. They are plain declarations in <code>:root</code>/<code>.dark</code>, so components pick up the new values everywhere, in both modes. For a one-off tweak, every component also takes a <code>Class</code> that wins over its own classes. After you change the palette, run <code>blaizio contrast</code> to check it against WCAG AA.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Theming/Override" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Adding your own tokens</h2>
        <p>A new token takes the same three additions to the tokens file: a light value in <code>:root</code>, a dark value in <code>.dark</code>, and a <code>@@theme inline</code> mapping so Tailwind generates its utilities.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Theming/NewToken" />
    </section>
</div>
